nd be out of the game. I cannot check. Raising with my pair is stupid. My best move here is to play it safe and fold. It makes the most sense.\n\n**Fold. Fold it is.**\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: The current board is 3h, Kc, 6h, 10s, 7d. I hold 6s and 5h, giving me a pair of 6s. This is a weak hand, as it falls behind any King, Ten, Seven, or pocket pair higher than 6s. The opponent raised pre-flop, checked the flop and turn, and then placed a large bet of 921 on the river (likely an overbet relative to the pot size of ~500). This betting line is polarized, representing either a hidden monster (like a straight with 8-9 or 4-5, or a slow-played set) or a bluff. However, calling a large bet with only a pair of 6s is very risky and yields poor pot odds. Without specific reads on the opponent's bluffing frequency this early in the game (Hand 1), folding is the safest and most mathematically sound option to preserve chips.\n\nReason: My hand (Pair of 6s) is too weak to call a large river bet. The board contains three overcards (K, 10, 7), meaning I lose to a vast majority of the opponent's value range. The risk of losing a significant portion of my stack on a marginal bluff-catch is not justified.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
