f I raise it to try and bluff, the board is just too dangerous. I\'d be trying to represent a flush or straight when I have no flush draw and a blocking J that prevents straight. They might call with a weak made hand and ruin the bluff. The opponent might call out of curiosity or stubbornness.\n\nIt is likely that the opponent has something better than me, or is bluffing, or has a worse made hand. The opponent range is just too wide to make a call at all EV positive.\n\nI\'m folding.  Gotta live to fight another hand, and this feels like a losing battle.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: My hand is Jack-high (best 5 cards: K-J-10-8-7). The board is K-10-7-5-2 with three clubs, making a flush possible. The opponent limped pre-flop, bet the flop (showing interest in the K-10-7 texture), checked the turn (when the flush arrived), and made a small value bet on the river. My hand has very little showdown value, beating only missed draws with lower ranks than Jack. The opponent's range likely consists of pairs (K, 10, 7) or a flush, most of which beat me. The pot odds (~3:1) are insufficient for a hero call with just K-High, and the pot is too small to justify a high-risk bluff raise representing the flush. Preserving chips is the best strategy here.\n\nReason: I have no made hand and lose to any pair or Ace-high, which are likely in the opponent's range given the betting history. Calling is -EV, and bluffing is too risky for the small pot size.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
